nd it's running on a regular  basis we have our login integrated into  the script we have that error Alert  Logic integrated in the event and event  error um connect will essentially blast  use blasted and send out an email to the  specified viewers and collaborators  that error email follows the template  that I showed you earlier and looks a  little something like this this is just  a dummy error email that we set up but  you see here the version is our Dev  pipeline you see the script name the  chunk name the error itself and then we  also have a spot for the log path there  so I'm going to turn the floor over to  Andy to cover how we took those log  files that were in Json base format and  turned them into something a little more  digestible  all right thank you Colby so now you  have your automated processes set up and  you have your email alerts for error  logging now let's say we get an email  saying that there's an error the next  step is to troubleshoot right  however errors rarely occur in isolation  in order to fix an error efficiently and  to prevent it from occurring again in  future States you want to be able to  find if this error is occurring in your  development and production environment  as this is an error occurred multiple  times throughout your processes  and so before the process monitoring app  what we would have to do  is we have to go through the error and  process logs and as Chloe mentioned  before these are in Json format and so  and there's probably hundreds to  thousands of lines of this so you'd be  pretty much abusing your control F to  try to find what errors uh occur and  where it occurred and that's also not to  mention  that since we're logging almost every  day we'd have to go through all these  files to see if there's like some kind  of historic uh pattern coming on from  these errors  and so going through all this I would  have created to sort of trying to eat a  melting ice cream sundae everything's  mixed together uh different ice cream  flavors are mixing and you might you're  you're tasting like two three different  flavors and you're not sure if you like  it or not it's very interesting however  our approach was to season in some art  our magic and make a more beautiful ice  cream sundae where all the components  are easily seeable and you can get to  where you need to be quickly and  efficiently  and so we did this with this was our  process for our process monitoring app  we took a bunch of messy logs  applied some login our shiny poly data  tables any R visualization packages  and turned it into a dashboard where we  can view both how our processes were  doing and the errors we got within one  single dashboard  however we first needed to be able to  read all of these logs into something  that was R readable  fortunately for us  log it provides this function already  there is a function called read  underscore logs where if you pass the  path of your error file it'll read in  that error file and return in our data  table that's easily filtered so now we  could filter based on if we wanted to  find an error and then by which specific  process and then with within a specific  time frame so this is very useful for  putting up the dashboard initially  and so we fed this data into a data  table call and built error data tables  where we could view all of our error  data errors in relation to each other  when they occurred and if they occurred  within any other processes  We additionally fit our process logs  into another data table and because we  had set up our login calls within the HR  markdown Chunk we can now see how long  each our markdown chunk took within the  process and be able to identify any  bottlenecks as needed  what we also did was we also fed some of  the process log data into  plotly and so we created this graph  where we could easily be able to see how  our processes were doing in relation to  each other and in relation to our  development and production environments